Nursemaid's Elbow
Paediatric & Neonatal
Refusal to use arm, History of axial traction, Arm held flexed/pronated

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PDN-034
Reason for Visit: Sudden, acute refusal to move the left arm following a pulling injury, requiring urgent evaluation to rule out a displaced supracondylar or radial fracture.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a highly distressed 3-year-old completely refusing to use their left arm after being swung by the hands. Immediate assessment was required to definitively exclude an acute fracture, assess neurovascular integrity, and perform a therapeutic manual reduction to restore function and alleviate severe pain prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I grabbed his hand to stop him running into the street, he yelped, and now he won't move his arm at all and just holds it against his stomach."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with an acute, sudden onset of complete refusal to physically use the left arm following a specific traction injury occurring 30 minutes ago. The clear, highly specific mechanism prominently involved sudden axial traction explicitly exerted upon the extended, pronated forearm when the father pulled the child back from the road. The affected arm is currently held rigidly, pronated, and explicitly slightly flexed strictly close to the child's lateral body wall. There is intense crying strictly provoked explicitly by any active or passive supination attempt. The child is otherwise well with no underlying generalized systemic infectious symptoms.
Functional Status: Distressed, functionally one-armed.
Social/Safety Concerns: Caregiver guilt regarding the mechanism of injury.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 95/60 | HR: 110 | Temp: 36.8C | RR: 22 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 15 Kg
General: Tearful, clutching the left arm against the abdomen, easily distracted by toys but guards the arm.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal inspection reveals a complete, absolute lack of profound bony swelling, severe deformity, or extensive localized bruising over the left elbow, wrist, or shoulder joint. Palpation of the clavicle and distal humerus elicits no pain. There is distinct tenderness explicitly localized strictly over the radial head and the annular ligament. Neurovascular status is completely intact distally (brisk capillary refill, normal radial pulse). The diagnostic tetrad of refusal to use the arm, history of axial traction, and the arm held flexed/pronated is explicitly confirmed.
Environmental Observations: Safe home environment; child is sitting on the caregiver's lap.
Medication Reconciliation: No medications administered.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Radial Head Subluxation (Nursemaid's Elbow) - Status: Acute displacement
Differentials:

Supracondylar Humerus Fracture - Ruled out explicitly by the clear traction (not fall on outstretched hand) mechanism, absolute lack of profound joint swelling, and the absence of visible gross deformity.

Clavicle Fracture - Ruled out by the lack of direct trauma to the shoulder, normal contour of the clavicle, and lack of pain upon direct palpation of the clavicle.

Septic Arthritis - Ruled out strictly by the acute traumatic onset, absence of fever, and lack of localized warmth or erythema.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The toddler's acute presentation of a sudden, rigid refusal to move the upper limb following a traction event necessitated an immediate after-hours assessment to safely rule out an occult fracture. Telephone triage accurately identified the classic mechanism for radial head subluxation but required physical evaluation to ensure neurovascular safety. Objective clinical evaluation confirmed the classic posturing and absence of swelling, safely excluding a supracondylar fracture or septic joint. Delaying this assessment would have left the child in severe, unnecessary pain and the parents in distress. Immediate clinical intervention allowed for the safe, on-site execution of a closed manual reduction, resulting in instantaneous, definitive cure and functional restoration without requiring traumatic hospital radiography.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Performed closed manual reduction via the hyperpronation technique. A palpable, explicit, and satisfying mechanical click was routinely felt directly over the radial head during the maneuver.
Positive Behavioral Change: Immediately following the reduction, hold out a highly preferred object (e.g., a brightly colored sticker or toy) toward the affected hand. The child explicitly receives the reward only when they independently reach out and grasp it with the previously injured arm. This immediately visually verifies the successful reduction for the clinician and positively reinforces the child's active realization that the arm is no longer painful to use.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review only required if the child stops using the arm again.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the arm suddenly swells up, becomes visibly deformed, turns pale or cold, or if the child develops a high fever.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Subluxations can easily recur; the ligament remains temporarily stretched.
Clinical Course: The pain is completely and instantaneously resolved. The child should resume perfectly normal, unrestricted play within 10 to 15 minutes of the reduction.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency medical attention if the hand becomes cold, blue, or numb.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A small ligament at the elbow temporarily slipped out of place when the arm was pulled. The click felt was the ligament snapping back into its correct, safe position. Nothing is broken or permanently damaged.
Actionable Steps: Absolutely do not lift, swing, or forcefully pull the child by the hands or wrists. Always lift the child securely from under the armpits to prevent this from happening again.

SMS INFORMATION

Diagnosis: Nursemaid's Elbow (Slipped ligament). Successfully reduced. Normal movement restored. No fracture suspected. Always lift child by armpits.

SMS EDUCATION

Offer a toy to the injured arm to encourage use. Do not swing child by the hands. Go to ED if severe swelling occurs.
